    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p13_b15",
      "text": "For each candidate we elicit a small directed graph of sub-\nclaims and support links with a deterministic template. Sani-\ntization removes self loops, merges paraphrases, and breaks\ncycles by minimal edge cut. Structural features include\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.44,
        671.59,
        291.1,
        717.49
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        221,
        2686,
        1164,
        2869
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.03,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 237,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p13_b16",
      "text": "minimal path coverage, cycle count, dangling nodes, contra-\ndiction score from a local natural language inference (NLI)\nverifier, and graph size. The topology score is a nonnega-\ntive linear combination tuned to match a target distribution\non the calibration split. Uncertainty has two components.\nEpistemic uncertainty samples K = 3 re elicited graphs per\ncandidate under minor prompt or temperature perturbations.\nWe compute variance of the topology score and JSD across\npath distributions. Aleatoric uncertainty uses node level\ncorrectness probabilities from a calibrated verifier and a\ncoverage corrected binary entropy with prior strength 0.05.\nPair uncertainty is averaged across the two candidates and\nmapped to a weight with \u03c4w = 1.2 and wmin = 0.05. Table\n15 shows the effect of the number of re elicited graphs on\ncalibration and throughput. We find K = 3 balances quality\nand speed.\n",
      "bbox": [
        306.69,
        69.54,
        543.19,
        258.76
